{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: more police and zero‑tolerance neighbourhood policing; 20,000 extra prison places with honesty in sentencing; empower heads to control admissions/expulsions and create Turnaround Schools; bring back Matron, invest in infection control, end waiting lists, support carers; British Border Control Police, Australian‑style points system, annual immigration limit and 24‑hour port surveillance; money/patient/pupil follows the user, tougher transparent exams, sack failing ministers, elected police commissioners; link basic pension to earnings; cut waste to lower taxes and red tape. TEXT_G: repeal the European Communities Act to leave the EU and restore supremacy of UK law; near‑zero net immigration with points system, Britishness/language tests and stricter removals; tougher sentencing, build prisons, more local police accountability; scrap EU‑derived regulations like the Working Time Directive; large immediate tax cuts and long‑term simplification (flat tax considered); NHS local control and private insurance options; ban GM crops until proven safe; repeal the Human Rights Act and scrap ID cards.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate’s highest weights on authority and care are better met by TEXT_F’s emphasis on domestic order (school discipline, policing, sentencing, border enforcement) alongside concrete care provisions (NHS cleanliness, ending waiting lists, support for carers) and proportionality/accountability (tougher exams, performance‑linked funding, sacking failing ministers). TEXT_G strongly stresses national sovereignty and immigration control (loyalty/authority against supranational bodies) and deregulation/tax cuts (proportionality) but gives comparatively less concrete emphasis to care, making it a looser fit to a profile that prioritizes authority with substantial care and only moderate loyalty and proportionality.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
